WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.242
He was the king of narco-political law enforcement corruption in Mexico.

00:00:06.561 --> 00:00:09.663
Carrillo was famous for the idea that if a load's lost,

00:00:09.663 --> 00:00:11.423
you kill everyone connected with it.

00:00:11.784 --> 00:00:12.903
That way you get the trade.

00:00:13.504 --> 00:00:16.824
He said that someone had to get their hands dirty for the sake of Mexico.

00:00:17.064 --> 00:00:18.425
And if that meant moving drugs,

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:18.666
well,

00:00:18.846 --> 00:00:19.385
let's move them.

00:00:51.292 --> 00:00:51.813
September 29th,

00:00:51.814 --> 00:00:53.213
1989.

00:00:53.774 --> 00:00:56.676
It was the largest seizure of cocaine the world had ever seen.

00:00:57.277 --> 00:00:58.518
Over 21 tons,

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:01.962
with a potential street value in excess of $7 billion,

00:01:02.281 --> 00:01:04.063
discovered in a warehouse in Sylmar,

00:01:04.143 --> 00:01:04.763
California.

00:01:05.525 --> 00:01:11.209
Boxes of the narcotic had been stacked 8 feet high alongside bags filled with over $10 million in cash.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:16.194
Investigators soon discovered that the drugs had entered the United States via the bridge that connects El Paso,

00:01:16.334 --> 00:01:18.876
Texas with the Mexican border community of Juarez.

00:01:20.024 --> 00:01:23.865
And while most law enforcement officials wouldn't learn his name for several more years,

00:01:24.445 --> 00:01:30.268
they were already experiencing the influence of a mysterious Mexican drug lord named Amado Carrillo Fuentes.

00:01:31.248 --> 00:01:38.231
His rise to power and ultimate demise offers a rare glimpse into the narco-political underworld of Mexican organized crime.

00:01:43.293 --> 00:01:43.573
He says,

00:01:43.575 --> 00:01:44.873
I want to tell you a name,

00:01:46.515 --> 00:01:48.415
Amado Carrillo Fuentes.

00:01:49.328 --> 00:01:49.608
He said,

00:01:49.628 --> 00:01:51.109
I want to tell you one other thing.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:53.011
Never say that name out loud.

00:01:53.791 --> 00:01:58.255
He was the king of narco-political law enforcement corruption in Mexico.

00:01:58.535 --> 00:02:02.758
There was a time when Amado was moving drugs in old commercial airliners.

00:02:03.419 --> 00:02:04.380
Moving it by the ton.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:05.280
You know,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.341
ten tons of coke.

00:02:07.002 --> 00:02:09.263
Pure Colombian coke in a passenger airline.

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:10.985
That's how he got his nickname.

00:02:11.925 --> 00:02:13.606
El Señor de los Cielos.

00:02:14.287 --> 00:02:15.147
Lord of the Skies.

00:02:21.245 --> 00:02:30.671
The 2,000 mile expanse of the Mexican-US border has been attracting smugglers since before Prohibition when men delivered whiskey to the thirsty patrons of Texas and Arizona.

00:02:31.872 --> 00:02:33.653
Marijuana was a drug of choice in the

00:02:34.014 --> 00:02:34.133
30s.

00:02:35.174 --> 00:02:36.195
After World War II,

00:02:36.316 --> 00:02:40.679
recovering GIs addicted to morphine created an enormous demand for heroin.

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:42.580
For more than a century,

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.864
Mexico's traficantes have catered to America's changing appetites.

00:02:48.812 --> 00:02:52.133
Smuggling activities are often coordinated from the state of Sinaloa,

00:02:52.493 --> 00:02:55.814
long recognized as Mexico's marijuana and heroin heartland.

00:02:56.495 --> 00:02:56.914
The U.S.

00:02:56.995 --> 00:03:00.997
Drug Enforcement Administration has even given the region a special nickname.

00:03:01.216 --> 00:03:03.557
A lot of the drug guys come out of that area.

00:03:03.598 --> 00:03:04.538
It's very poor,

00:03:05.139 --> 00:03:05.478
you know,

00:03:05.538 --> 00:03:06.598
it's a lot of ranchos.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.721
And so DEA calls it Jurassic Park,

00:03:09.881 --> 00:03:12.841
because that's where the prehistoric monsters come from.

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:15.543
Charles Bowden is a journalist and author.

00:03:16.002 --> 00:03:16.503
His book,

00:03:16.843 --> 00:03:17.264
Juarez.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.921
The laboratory of our future takes a critical look at the forces shaping modern Mexico.

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:26.305
You have to understand Sinaloa has always had a bandit culture,

00:03:26.485 --> 00:03:27.205
hereditary.

00:03:27.604 --> 00:03:27.765
It's,

00:03:27.786 --> 00:03:28.026
you know,

00:03:28.026 --> 00:03:29.445
an entire state with a

00:03:29.907 --> 00:03:31.146
Jesse James ethic.

00:03:32.427 --> 00:03:32.747
Indeed,

00:03:32.748 --> 00:03:36.689
the tradition of smuggling is so strong that the region has its own patron saint.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:43.213
Jesus Malverde was a bandit at the turn of the century with a Zorro-like reputation for helping the poor.

00:03:44.134 --> 00:03:47.096
After his execution by government forces in 1909...

00:03:47.788 --> 00:03:50.689
Villagers began to attribute miracles to the bandit martyr.

00:03:51.329 --> 00:03:57.192
Traffickers often visit his shrine to have bullets blessed or to thank the narco saint for their good fortune.

00:04:00.474 --> 00:04:04.276
It was into this environment that Amado Carrillo Fuentes was born in the early

00:04:05.237 --> 00:04:07.299
1950s. We know little of his childhood,

00:04:07.459 --> 00:04:07.978
but in the early

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:09.560
1970s, his uncle,

00:04:09.719 --> 00:04:10.781
Ernesto Fonseca,

00:04:11.381 --> 00:04:13.102
gave him a job harvesting marijuana.

00:04:14.122 --> 00:04:16.423
Carrillo was starting his career at the very bottom.

00:04:18.975 --> 00:04:26.540
Fonseca was a powerful smuggler and original member of the influential Guadalajara cartel under the tough Miguel Angel Feliz Gallardo.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.682
A former Sinaloa state police officer,

00:04:30.022 --> 00:04:33.464
Miguel campaigned for cooperation among the competing traffickers,

00:04:33.884 --> 00:04:37.046
a philosophy Carrillo himself would adopt many years later.

00:04:39.147 --> 00:04:45.151
This spirit of cooperation put the cartel in a unique position to benefit from another organization's misfortune.

00:04:45.348 --> 00:04:48.950
They said,

00:04:48.951 --> 00:04:49.031
Sean,

00:04:49.031 --> 00:04:49.632
they're running three people.

00:04:49.632 --> 00:04:49.932
Four people total.

00:04:49.932 --> 00:04:50.372
Four people total.

00:04:50.372 --> 00:04:51.392
In 1982,

00:04:51.394 --> 00:04:51.432
U.S.

00:04:51.433 --> 00:04:57.317
anti-drug forces deployed throughout South Florida and crippled the distribution network of Columbia's cocaine campaign.

00:05:00.692 --> 00:05:01.793
Stung by the losses,

00:05:01.973 --> 00:05:05.735
the Colombians needed a reliable way to move their product into the U.S.

00:05:07.016 --> 00:05:08.918
Miguel Angel jumped at the opportunity.

00:05:09.497 --> 00:05:12.319
The cartel had ferryed cocaine through Mexico before,

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.763
but this new arrangement would encompass much larger loads and bring much greater profits,

00:05:17.244 --> 00:05:19.025
up to $2,000 per kilo.

00:05:19.845 --> 00:05:24.288
It wasn't long before the Sinaloa drug lords were making more money than ever before.

00:05:24.809 --> 00:05:29.432
the Sierra Madre where there's no electricity and people have thatched roofs.

00:05:29.963 --> 00:05:34.866
You start seeing nightclubs of lavish marvel and things in the middle of nowhere.

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:38.208
And then you notice that people that are barefoot have Uzis.

00:05:39.408 --> 00:05:40.609
And what are you supposed to think?

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:41.910
They had a good corn crop?

00:05:43.631 --> 00:05:48.213
It is unknown how Carrillo made the transition from harvesting marijuana to full-out trafficking.

00:05:49.093 --> 00:05:54.115
Most believe that Carrillo had completed his apprenticeship and was ready for a larger role in the organization.

00:05:54.783 --> 00:05:59.947
That larger role may well have included a five-year stint as a member of Mexico's secret police,

00:06:00.207 --> 00:06:01.228
the DFS.

00:06:01.749 --> 00:06:04.471
DFS stands for the Directorate of Federal Security,

00:06:05.051 --> 00:06:09.715
and it was the secret police agency of Gobernación,

00:06:09.775 --> 00:06:12.277
which is the interior government ministry of Mexico.

00:06:12.758 --> 00:06:14.719
Amado had one of those badges.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:15.480
He was an agent.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.543
At the time of the bust of the Buffalo marijuana complex in

00:06:20.304 --> 00:06:21.124
Chihuahua, Mexico,

00:06:21.825 --> 00:06:24.007
a vast multi-billion dollar operation.

00:06:24.267 --> 00:06:28.868
which at the time employed 7,000 farm workers on the marijuana fields.

00:06:29.148 --> 00:06:32.049
The biggest drug plantation ever created on Earth.

00:06:32.449 --> 00:06:35.510
One so large it could be seen by American satellites.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.952
The drug complex was exposed by DEA agent Enrique Camarena,

00:06:40.252 --> 00:06:41.072
who was kidnapped,

00:06:41.172 --> 00:06:41.592
tortured,

00:06:41.593 --> 00:06:44.333
and murdered by leaders of the Guadalajara cartel,

00:06:44.913 --> 00:06:46.314
Carrillo's uncle among them.

00:06:46.454 --> 00:06:48.314
In the investigations that came out later,

00:06:48.714 --> 00:06:51.235
it turned out that the DFS was...

00:06:51.755 --> 00:06:54.897
Deeply involved in the protection of this operation along with the MFJP,

00:06:55.057 --> 00:06:56.079
the Mexican Federal Police,

00:06:56.579 --> 00:06:57.319
and the Mexican Army.

00:06:57.919 --> 00:07:01.701
The cowardly murder of Enrique Kike Camarena,

00:07:02.502 --> 00:07:04.843
special agent with the Drug Enforcement Administration,

00:07:06.224 --> 00:07:10.427
was carried out by the Mexican government in conjunction,

00:07:11.147 --> 00:07:11.988
in cooperation,

00:07:12.389 --> 00:07:16.831
in partnership with the godfathers of the drug trade.

00:07:17.208 --> 00:07:20.729
Phil Jordan retired from the DEA after 30 years of service,

00:07:20.809 --> 00:07:24.191
including 10 years as special agent in charge of the Dallas office,

00:07:24.251 --> 00:07:25.611
and one year as head of EPIC,

00:07:26.092 --> 00:07:28.312
DEA's El Paso Intelligence Center,

00:07:28.653 --> 00:07:31.874
the largest database of drug trafficking information in the world.

00:07:33.094 --> 00:07:35.075
He and Camarena were good friends.

00:07:35.515 --> 00:07:36.056
To this day,

00:07:36.296 --> 00:07:43.319
Mexico has never apologized for having captured and delivered Tiki Camarena to the traffickers.

00:07:43.599 --> 00:07:46.800
Because of the international pressures that resulted from...

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:48.021
mainly from the United States,

00:07:48.821 --> 00:07:52.284
the DFS dismissed 400 of its agents.

00:07:53.045 --> 00:07:56.608
And I have to assume that Amado was one of those 400 who were dismissed at that time.

00:07:56.748 --> 00:07:59.850
In 1985,

00:08:00.291 --> 00:08:02.753
Carrillo made his way to the border town of Ojinaga,

00:08:03.253 --> 00:08:10.799
a building situated near the Big Bend region of Texas that was rapidly becoming a major distribution center for narcotics in northern Mexico.

00:08:11.039 --> 00:08:13.041
It had always been very important for marijuana and heroin,

00:08:13.882 --> 00:08:16.564
but after the cocaine smuggling shifted from...

00:08:16.887 --> 00:08:17.487
The Caribbean,

00:08:18.068 --> 00:08:18.628
in New Mexico,

00:08:18.988 --> 00:08:20.909
Ojinaga became cocaine central.

00:08:21.411 --> 00:08:22.070
At the time,

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:25.392
Pablo Acosta was the undisputed crime boss of Ojinaga.

00:08:26.353 --> 00:08:30.075
Having smuggled heroin and marijuana in the region for close to 15 years,

00:08:30.415 --> 00:08:32.336
he had finally taken control of the region,

00:08:32.456 --> 00:08:33.018
or plaza,

00:08:33.217 --> 00:08:34.197
three years previous.

00:08:35.678 --> 00:08:37.519
In a process known as piggybacking,

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:42.502
the Guadalajara cartel had added cocaine to Acosta's well-respected operations.

00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:43.884
Pablo's strength...

00:08:44.148 --> 00:08:46.928
was that he had the protection soda for that whole region.

00:08:47.288 --> 00:08:49.229
And so the cocaine coming in was secure.

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:50.350
Nobody had to worry about it.

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:51.590
If it was in Pablo's hands,

00:08:51.591 --> 00:08:52.149
it was safe.

00:08:52.629 --> 00:08:56.492
Proof that Carrillo was an Ohinaga came in 1985 when U.S.

00:08:56.572 --> 00:09:00.013
Customs agent detained him for carrying an automatic rifle into Mexico.

00:09:03.013 --> 00:09:05.614
This was Carrillo's first and only run-in with U.S.

00:09:05.673 --> 00:09:06.413
law enforcement,

00:09:06.795 --> 00:09:11.776
and it produced the oldest known photographs of the man who had become Mexico's most powerful drug lord.

00:09:13.399 --> 00:09:15.701
Carrillo spoke freely with the customs officials,

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.281
giving them a great deal of personal information.

00:09:19.201 --> 00:09:20.162
And even though U.S.

00:09:20.242 --> 00:09:23.743
intelligence had already identified Carrillo as a powerful trafficker,

00:09:24.182 --> 00:09:27.884
they did not know how the Sinaloan fit into Pablo Acosta's organization.

00:09:28.764 --> 00:09:31.245
They seemed at least to have had a good friendship.

00:09:32.325 --> 00:09:32.665
Pablo,

00:09:32.705 --> 00:09:33.384
when I was with him,

00:09:33.386 --> 00:09:35.365
was very proud of the ingot around his neck.

00:09:35.406 --> 00:09:36.365
I asked him about that.

00:09:36.466 --> 00:09:38.706
It was a one-ounce gold ingot.

00:09:38.708 --> 00:09:40.227
He said it was a gift of a model,

00:09:40.228 --> 00:09:41.167
and then he showed his watch.

00:09:41.227 --> 00:09:42.868
It was a solid gold Rolex.

00:09:43.552 --> 00:09:43.971
That also,

00:09:44.153 --> 00:09:44.653
according to him,

00:09:44.673 --> 00:09:45.854
had been a gift of Amado.

00:09:46.575 --> 00:09:48.115
He warred everywhere.

00:09:48.176 --> 00:09:48.495
He was,

00:09:49.517 --> 00:09:49.856
like I said,

00:09:49.876 --> 00:09:50.457
very proud of that.

00:09:50.458 --> 00:09:50.677
In fact,

00:09:50.697 --> 00:09:51.239
when he died,

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.441
he had the same watch around his wrist.

00:09:54.721 --> 00:09:59.926
Carrillo's arrival coincided with a sudden upswing in the amount of cocaine being flown into Ojinaga.

00:10:00.947 --> 00:10:04.350
Experts now believe that Carrillo's role at the border was a dual one,

00:10:05.009 --> 00:10:11.035
to coordinate his uncle's Colombian shipments and to apprentice him with Mexico's most experienced smugglers.

00:10:25.265 --> 00:10:28.906
Pablo Acosta was an expert at getting drugs across.

00:10:28.926 --> 00:10:29.706
He knew how to do it,

00:10:29.826 --> 00:10:30.406
and he taught

00:10:30.867 --> 00:10:31.927
Amado everything he knew.

00:10:33.207 --> 00:10:34.428
Amado was a little bit smarter,

00:10:34.468 --> 00:10:34.648
though.

00:10:35.908 --> 00:10:39.069
Pablo Acosta used to freebase cocaine and do all this stuff.

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:39.289
I mean,

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:39.549
he was,

00:10:39.989 --> 00:10:41.670
even if he hadn't gotten killed in a shootout,

00:10:41.671 --> 00:10:43.130
he was on his way to an early grave.

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:44.610
Aging and addicted,

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:46.751
Acosta was losing his grip on the plaza.

00:10:47.891 --> 00:10:48.191
Carrillo,

00:10:48.291 --> 00:10:49.032
by contrast,

00:10:49.033 --> 00:10:54.053
was coming into his own after his uncle's arrest for the murder of Special Agent Camarena.

00:10:54.621 --> 00:10:56.202
It is possible to assume that Cutty,

00:10:56.222 --> 00:10:57.982
who inherited his uncle's operation,

00:10:59.103 --> 00:11:00.443
in late 1986,

00:11:00.704 --> 00:11:04.505
Terrence Popper met both men and found the contrast memorable.

00:11:09.347 --> 00:11:12.188
We were sitting there in an apartment above a radio station.

00:11:12.668 --> 00:11:13.769
There was a long table there,

00:11:14.549 --> 00:11:17.010
and there was ashtrays overflowing with cigarette butts,

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:18.011
or machine gun clips,

00:11:18.031 --> 00:11:18.511
or it was his

00:11:19.311 --> 00:11:20.632
AR-15 right behind him.

00:11:21.152 --> 00:11:23.073
He had a .45 in his belt.

00:11:24.089 --> 00:11:32.531
At some point he pulled out a little bag of cocaine and then a box of baking soda and mixed the two up and then cooked it over a flame.

00:11:32.771 --> 00:11:33.912
And it melted,

00:11:33.952 --> 00:11:36.072
it bubbled up and then fused together.

00:11:36.772 --> 00:11:40.774
And for the rest of the night he was either scraping that stuff into a powder,

00:11:41.674 --> 00:11:43.314
making packs of cigarettes,

00:11:44.915 --> 00:11:46.215
smoke that he kept in his pocket,

00:11:46.835 --> 00:11:47.755
or smoking the stuff.

00:11:48.916 --> 00:11:53.577
Everybody I've talked to about him attributes his decline and fall to cocaine.

00:11:54.413 --> 00:11:55.494
And then Amato came over.

00:11:56.195 --> 00:11:57.016
He walked in the room,

00:11:57.036 --> 00:11:58.477
he was dressed in western,

00:11:58.517 --> 00:11:59.037
I recall.

00:11:59.038 --> 00:11:59.518
You know,

00:11:59.519 --> 00:12:00.398
a little bit pudgy,

00:12:01.379 --> 00:12:03.581
friendly.

00:12:03.582 --> 00:12:04.802
A little bit overly friendly,

00:12:04.822 --> 00:12:05.202
in fact.

00:12:05.963 --> 00:12:06.844
There was one thing I remember,

00:12:06.884 --> 00:12:07.104
though,

00:12:07.264 --> 00:12:08.245
that really stands out,

00:12:08.925 --> 00:12:11.648
and that was a sort of a glint in his eyes.

00:12:13.329 --> 00:12:19.614
And I think he knew the fact that Pablo was talking to an American reporter spelled his doom.

00:12:20.054 --> 00:12:21.195
I think Amato knew that.

00:12:21.856 --> 00:12:23.037
He was aiming to take over.

00:12:23.721 --> 00:12:25.363
And taking over meant getting rid of Pablo.

00:12:27.304 --> 00:12:30.588
We will never know if Carrillo ever challenges Acosta's authority directly.

00:12:30.608 --> 00:12:32.329
But in 1986,

00:12:32.469 --> 00:12:35.532
the two men exchanged heated words at a restaurant in Okinawa.

00:12:36.273 --> 00:12:40.976
Pablo and Amado's operation included a ranch not too far out of town,

00:12:40.977 --> 00:12:41.816
maybe 20 miles,

00:12:42.417 --> 00:12:44.218
where there was an underground storage tank.

00:12:44.238 --> 00:12:44.578
It's like,

00:12:44.898 --> 00:12:45.158
you know,

00:12:45.159 --> 00:12:46.239
an old gasoline tank,

00:12:46.259 --> 00:12:46.499
you know,

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:47.700
next to a runway.

00:13:06.652 --> 00:13:17.459
and then offload it and bury it in a tank and only the traffickers knew about this tank somehow or other the DEA found out about it

00:13:17.659 --> 00:13:31.813
this tank somehow or other amato found out that the dea knew about it now in the early of pablo amato had six people rounded up who had knowledge of that underground tank and he had each one of them tortured to death

00:13:32.593 --> 00:13:34.914
to find out if there was a leak or if they knew anything about a leak.

00:13:36.956 --> 00:13:37.556
None of them did,

00:13:38.176 --> 00:13:38.937
but they were all dead.

00:13:41.118 --> 00:13:42.619
Acosta confronted Carrillo,

00:13:42.859 --> 00:13:45.820
asking him what he had accomplished by killing six innocent men.

00:13:46.841 --> 00:13:47.681
Carrillo replied,

00:13:48.002 --> 00:13:49.903
coolly,

00:13:49.983 --> 00:13:55.966
It is better for six innocent men to die than for one guilty man to go unpunished.

00:13:55.986 --> 00:13:58.247
I think Amado learned some lessons from Pablo.

00:13:58.547 --> 00:14:01.109
He didn't learn the crack cocaine lesson very well because he...

00:14:01.529 --> 00:14:04.151
appeared to do that for the rest of his career.

00:14:05.872 --> 00:14:09.835
But he did learn that the high profile had some real strong drawbacks.

00:14:11.796 --> 00:14:13.517
During his interview with Terrence Popper,

00:14:13.857 --> 00:14:18.240
Pablo had revealed part of the protection scheme unique to Mexico's organized crime groups.

00:14:19.001 --> 00:14:20.342
In order to control the plot,

00:14:20.582 --> 00:14:24.365
he had to make regular payments to both the Army and the Mexican federal police.

00:14:25.425 --> 00:14:27.026
As soon as the interview was published,

00:14:27.307 --> 00:14:30.569
the order came from Mexico City to arrest Pablo Acosta.

00:14:31.037 --> 00:14:33.638
For the next five months they chased him all over Mexico.

00:14:33.678 --> 00:14:36.959
He ended up hiding out in a river village on the Rio Grande.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:42.382
And he was trapped in an adobe hut for about an hour.

00:14:42.422 --> 00:14:45.303
They had a hellacious gun battle and he was killed.

00:14:45.403 --> 00:14:46.243
He refused to give up.

00:14:47.484 --> 00:14:48.664
When Acosta went on the lam,

00:14:49.024 --> 00:14:50.025
Carrillo disappeared.

00:14:50.905 --> 00:14:53.266
Some believe he was involved in Acosta's death.

00:14:54.006 --> 00:14:55.147
I can't speak for the DEA.

00:14:55.407 --> 00:14:57.868
I can speak for the intelligence that was known.

00:14:58.668 --> 00:15:00.649
And the intelligence was that...

00:15:02.977 --> 00:15:08.269
Amado Carrillo Fuentes paid the Mexican federal police a million dollars to have

00:15:09.071 --> 00:15:09.872
Pablo Acosta killed.

00:15:10.312 --> 00:15:11.813
Never have a high profile.

00:15:11.953 --> 00:15:13.274
Don't talk to reporters.

00:15:13.314 --> 00:15:14.815
Don't let anyone know your name.

00:15:15.195 --> 00:15:17.357
Kill people that publicize your name.

00:15:19.058 --> 00:15:20.639
Because that's what killed Acosta.

00:15:21.259 --> 00:15:22.840
He became too well known,

00:15:23.361 --> 00:15:27.564
thereby becoming an embarrassment to the Mexican government that employed him.

00:15:28.724 --> 00:15:29.465
Which they did.

00:15:30.906 --> 00:15:34.849
The police and the government in Mexico get a cut of everything,

00:15:34.929 --> 00:15:35.889
including crime.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:38.091
So if you want to be a criminal...

00:15:38.699 --> 00:15:39.520
The head criminal,

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:43.583
you have to establish that you're in charge of the plaza,

00:15:43.824 --> 00:15:45.725
that you represent the criminal element.

00:15:46.566 --> 00:15:49.528
Then you can go to the comandante and cut a deal.

00:15:50.149 --> 00:15:51.550
And the comandante will tell you,

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:52.851
you can do anything you want,

00:15:53.032 --> 00:15:54.273
but if it gets to be trouble,

00:15:54.633 --> 00:15:55.454
I'm going to kill you,

00:15:55.794 --> 00:15:56.455
arrest you,

00:15:56.615 --> 00:15:57.135
jail you.

00:15:58.256 --> 00:15:59.978
But if that doesn't happen,

00:16:00.178 --> 00:16:02.860
you just give me my bike and you can do business.

00:16:03.921 --> 00:16:06.003
That was the way the plaza system functioned.

00:16:06.343 --> 00:16:06.883
Over time,

00:16:07.283 --> 00:16:11.285
it was natural for individual plazas to band together into larger alliances.

00:16:11.845 --> 00:16:13.286
Carrillo's original member,

00:16:13.386 --> 00:16:15.647
Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo,

00:16:15.667 --> 00:16:17.848
expanded his operations north of the border,

00:16:18.208 --> 00:16:19.609
forming the Tijuana Cartel.

00:16:19.629 --> 00:16:27.412
Juan García Obregón consolidated coastal smuggling with prohibition routes across the mountains to create the Gulf Cartel.

00:16:28.392 --> 00:16:32.094
The Juárez Cartel was under the control of Rafael Aguilar Gallardo,

00:16:32.714 --> 00:16:34.395
a former commander of the DFS.

00:16:35.039 --> 00:16:35.859
...Ria's alma mater.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:40.341
It is from Juarez that Carrillo resumed his air shipments from Colombia,

00:16:40.941 --> 00:16:44.222
mere days after Pablo Acosta's funeral.

00:16:44.982 --> 00:16:47.683
Aguilar was a federal secret policeman,

00:16:47.684 --> 00:16:50.904
giving unique access to the workings of traffickers throughout the region.

00:16:51.584 --> 00:16:55.505
Information most likely turned to his advantage in growing his cartel's influence.

00:16:56.305 --> 00:17:01.987
It is believed that Aguilar had an existing cocaine deal with Colombia's Cali cartel before Carrillo came to Juarez.

00:17:02.923 --> 00:17:07.026
Carillo's connection to the median traffic is a welcome addition to the business.

00:17:08.748 --> 00:17:10.649
Then in August of 1989,

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:12.431
Carillo was arrested in Guadalajara,

00:17:12.911 --> 00:17:14.733
charged with trafficking for the Colombian.

00:17:15.714 --> 00:17:18.156
The military confiscated six of his aircraft,

00:17:18.576 --> 00:17:19.337
four of them jets.

00:17:20.377 --> 00:17:21.919
He was released a few months later.

00:17:22.299 --> 00:17:23.140
He was arrested,

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:23.400
though.

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:31.907
A photograph was taken of him that was distinctly different from the photograph taken of him in Ojinaga when he applied for a border crossing card.

00:17:32.631 --> 00:17:39.136
The only way that I could think of for a person to radically alter his appearance like that is through plastic surgery.

00:17:40.558 --> 00:17:44.901
Correa was in prison when authorities discovered the 21 tons of cocaine in Sylmar,

00:17:44.961 --> 00:17:45.602
California.

00:17:46.402 --> 00:17:50.946
It is likely that the young drug lord had helped transport the narcotics into northern Mexico.

00:17:51.487 --> 00:17:54.429
I was in charge of the El Paso office at the time that it happened.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:58.712
We had an idea that there was a lot of narcotics coming across.

00:18:00.014 --> 00:18:01.455
We did not know the...

00:18:02.391 --> 00:18:06.012
tonnage which came all through the El Paso corridor.

00:18:06.372 --> 00:18:08.933
It was all through the Amado Carrillo Fuentes's organization.

00:18:09.553 --> 00:18:20.416
DEA in an intercept heard a conversation between a known drug guy in LA and a drug guy in Colombia and the Colombian said they didn't get the other 20 tons did they.

00:18:22.677 --> 00:18:30.639
Sylmar terrified the American drug community because it blew the whistle on how big the volume of the traffic was.

00:18:31.855 --> 00:18:32.535
And secondly,

00:18:32.975 --> 00:18:36.557
because they took it down and it never affected the price of coke on the street.

00:18:37.277 --> 00:18:39.098
By the spring of 1990,

00:18:39.099 --> 00:18:43.600
Carrillo was out of prison and Guerra's cartel was experiencing a period of relative calm.

00:18:44.981 --> 00:18:45.821
In contrast,

00:18:46.081 --> 00:18:48.282
western Mexico was becoming a war zone.

00:18:50.443 --> 00:18:53.444
Shortly after the arrest of Miguel Angel Feliz Gallardo,

00:18:53.445 --> 00:18:55.285
the Tijuana cartel split.

00:18:56.686 --> 00:18:59.687
Joaquin El Chapo Guzman and Hector Luis Palma.

00:19:00.087 --> 00:19:01.888
formed the rival Sinaloa Cartel.

00:19:03.168 --> 00:19:04.428
Miguel Angel's nephews,

00:19:04.648 --> 00:19:05.749
the Arellano brothers,

00:19:05.789 --> 00:19:08.529
arranged the revenge murder of Palma's wife and children.

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:16.211
The Sinaloa Cartel retaliated by sending 40 armed gunmen to attack a discotheque where one of the Arellanos was partying.

00:19:16.592 --> 00:19:22.973
The bloodbath culminated in the 1993 assassination of Cardinal Juan Jesus Posada Cipriani,

00:19:23.533 --> 00:19:26.154
killed in a fire fight at Guadalajara Airport.

00:19:27.435 --> 00:19:28.235
What that killing did,

00:19:28.255 --> 00:19:28.595
it...

00:19:30.111 --> 00:19:31.972
It outraged Mexican society,

00:19:33.032 --> 00:19:39.054
and it showed just how brazen traffickers had become.

00:19:39.514 --> 00:19:44.475
It showed the horrible possibilities of Mexico becoming another Colombia.

00:19:44.855 --> 00:19:48.136
The Juarez cartel took tremendous advantage of this uncivil war,

00:19:48.897 --> 00:19:53.258
expanding their territory and offering asylum to several traffickers caught in the crossfire.

00:19:54.098 --> 00:19:56.739
Even the Colombians found themselves having to make compromise.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:03.645
When Carrillo decided Mexicans wouldn't simply take a fee to ferry Colombian coke,

00:20:04.185 --> 00:20:05.867
but would move it for half the load.

00:20:06.988 --> 00:20:08.529
Because then when they took half the load,

00:20:08.549 --> 00:20:12.933
they started displacing Colombians in the United States in the distribution system.

00:20:13.613 --> 00:20:16.396
And that's when the roar of money hit Mexico.

00:20:16.756 --> 00:20:20.179
And that happened between 85 and 90,

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:22.521
depending who's accounting where.

00:20:23.402 --> 00:20:25.784
But the real architect was Amado Carrillo.

00:20:27.181 --> 00:20:28.840
Kid had a head for business,

00:20:28.841 --> 00:20:29.902
and he had a cold heart.

00:20:38.624 --> 00:20:41.243
The Rio streamlined the operation almost immediately,

00:20:41.563 --> 00:20:44.284
bringing the loose-knit cartel into the modern corporate era.

00:20:45.304 --> 00:20:50.366
Use cargo planes and passenger jets to transport 10 to 15 tons of cocaine at a time.

00:20:51.326 --> 00:20:52.466
His pipe...

00:20:52.506 --> 00:20:57.029
Pilots filed flight plans that blended the contraband into legitimate commercial air traffic.

00:20:57.850 --> 00:20:59.411
If the flights were detected at all,

00:20:59.732 --> 00:21:03.314
Mexico's anti-drug aircraft would be left breathing jet fuels.

00:21:04.175 --> 00:21:07.578
It wasn't long before Carrillo was given his most famous alias,

00:21:08.158 --> 00:21:11.881
Lord of the Skies.

00:21:11.882 --> 00:21:15.884
Carrillo's growing legend made it impossible for him to remain hidden.

00:21:15.885 --> 00:21:17.605
In November of 1993,

00:21:17.606 --> 00:21:21.148
he survived the only serious attempt ever known to have been made on his life.

00:21:21.892 --> 00:21:26.756
Amado Carrillo and his wife and children were eating in the evening at a fish joint.

00:21:26.936 --> 00:21:27.497
Nice one.

00:21:28.137 --> 00:21:30.239
Suddenly a bunch of guys stormed in,

00:21:30.559 --> 00:21:31.480
started shooting.

00:21:41.589 --> 00:21:43.030
Carrillo escaped.

00:21:43.631 --> 00:21:44.992
It's believed by DEA.

00:21:46.224 --> 00:21:52.752
That the reason Creel survived was he'd been careful enough that even his assassins didn't know what he looked like and shot the wrong man.

00:21:53.636 --> 00:21:55.457
It is still unknown who ordered the hit.

00:21:56.117 --> 00:22:02.040
Some experts say it was Gulf cartel leader Juan Garcia Abreg trying to eliminate his main competition.

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:09.023
Others point to the Arellano brothers in Tijuana who were upset because Carrillo had flooded money to their bitter rival,

00:22:09.024 --> 00:22:10.463
Joaquin El Chapo Guzman.

00:22:10.464 --> 00:22:12.204
They have a saying in Spanish,

00:22:12.704 --> 00:22:13.945
plata o plomo.

00:22:14.265 --> 00:22:15.906
That means silver or lead.

00:22:16.626 --> 00:22:17.906
You take the money or you get a bullet.

00:22:18.887 --> 00:22:21.068
And that's how these guys operate.

00:22:21.488 --> 00:22:23.369
Carrillo paid his loyal followers well.

00:22:23.929 --> 00:22:24.470
Conversely,

00:22:24.471 --> 00:22:26.491
he showed little mercy for the disobedient.

00:22:27.431 --> 00:22:28.452
And security leaks,

00:22:28.492 --> 00:22:30.013
intentional or accidental,

00:22:30.093 --> 00:22:32.775
were treated with the same swift surgical response.

00:22:34.135 --> 00:22:37.217
Carrillo was famous for the idea that if a load's lost,

00:22:37.237 --> 00:22:38.978
you kill everyone connected with it.

00:22:39.318 --> 00:22:40.499
That way you get the traitor.

00:22:41.379 --> 00:22:41.980
And he did it.

00:22:43.361 --> 00:22:49.044
Carrillo took in 93 and 94 to killing people where he wanted the bodies found.

00:22:49.704 --> 00:22:52.285
And we're tying them with yellow ribbons as a message.

00:22:52.965 --> 00:22:53.485
Gradually,

00:22:53.545 --> 00:22:54.946
Amado progressed.

00:22:54.947 --> 00:22:57.606
He apparently owned the only tiger in Juarez.

00:22:58.167 --> 00:23:01.488
There's a history among drug guys of liking to own big predators.

00:23:01.908 --> 00:23:02.788
In 96,

00:23:02.868 --> 00:23:03.968
the tiger escaped,

00:23:04.109 --> 00:23:04.549
actually,

00:23:04.609 --> 00:23:06.289
and ran around in the streets there.

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:09.050
It was captured and sent to Ciudad Chihuahua,

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:09.770
the capital,

00:23:09.790 --> 00:23:11.091
to be placed in that zoo.

00:23:12.031 --> 00:23:13.051
About that time,

00:23:13.671 --> 00:23:15.252
drug killing started appearing.

00:23:16.028 --> 00:23:17.089
Where the people would be dead,

00:23:17.169 --> 00:23:17.790
executed,

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:18.470
hands tied,

00:23:18.530 --> 00:23:19.311
shot through the head,

00:23:19.691 --> 00:23:24.495
but would be delicately draped with these cheap tapestries with tigers on them.

00:23:25.296 --> 00:23:26.977
If you're found in this fashion,

00:23:27.357 --> 00:23:28.518
it's a send a message.

00:23:29.039 --> 00:23:30.500
It's a form of policing.

00:23:31.501 --> 00:23:32.362
And it was rigorous,

00:23:32.382 --> 00:23:33.803
and Carrillo was very good at it.

00:23:35.504 --> 00:23:36.485
Under Carrillo's reign,

00:23:36.725 --> 00:23:38.467
Juarez was a city under siege,

00:23:38.627 --> 00:23:40.268
choking on the violence of its ruler.

00:23:41.669 --> 00:23:42.510
In contrast,

00:23:42.670 --> 00:23:44.852
the drug lord's hometown of Guamuchilito...

00:23:45.072 --> 00:23:46.914
benefited from the drug lord's largesse.

00:23:48.395 --> 00:23:50.337
He was very good with all of the people.

00:23:51.458 --> 00:23:52.399
What did he do here,

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:53.220
for example?

00:23:55.021 --> 00:23:57.103
The main thing he did was build the church.

00:23:57.864 --> 00:23:58.845
The locals there love him.

00:23:59.525 --> 00:24:01.246
For people who live in that area,

00:24:01.627 --> 00:24:02.327
poor farmers,

00:24:03.448 --> 00:24:03.869
he's money,

00:24:04.569 --> 00:24:05.050
he's wealth,

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:07.332
and he's a father figure.

00:24:09.662 --> 00:24:11.884
In late December of 1994,

00:24:12.263 --> 00:24:15.165
the value of Mexico's currency on the open market plummeted.

00:24:16.145 --> 00:24:17.446
Like most drug traffickers,

00:24:17.527 --> 00:24:19.127
Carrillo kept his fortune in U.S.

00:24:19.208 --> 00:24:20.990
dollars and soon found himself 30%

00:24:21.149 --> 00:24:21.349
richer.

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:24.791
In 1995,

00:24:24.811 --> 00:24:28.094
events conspired to make Carrillo's empire even more profitable.

00:24:29.535 --> 00:24:32.317
Colombian police arrested the Rodriguez Otohuela brothers.

00:24:32.816 --> 00:24:35.378
Carrillo's primary contacts in the Cali Cartel.

00:24:36.499 --> 00:24:38.661
With the Median Cartel in similar disarray,

00:24:39.001 --> 00:24:43.365
Carrillo arranged for the direct purchase of cocaine from farmers in Bolivia and Peru.

00:24:44.386 --> 00:24:49.991
What Carrillo really did was become almost the single broker of cocaine.

00:24:50.632 --> 00:24:52.874
And he held two meetings with the other capos,

00:24:52.954 --> 00:24:53.975
heads of cartels,

00:24:54.435 --> 00:24:56.397
and he hears the offer,

00:24:56.457 --> 00:24:58.058
I'll broker the cocaine,

00:24:58.118 --> 00:24:59.459
we don't fight over terrorism.

00:25:00.260 --> 00:25:01.021
You just come to me,

00:25:01.401 --> 00:25:02.623
I'll sell it to you wholesale,

00:25:03.023 --> 00:25:04.124
I won't try and hog it,

00:25:04.665 --> 00:25:09.590
and then we just all have our little thing as far as distribution in the United States and we live in peace.

00:25:10.471 --> 00:25:14.416
Because Mamado Crea was smart enough to know that war is expensive and peace is.

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:18.472
The Colombians recognized the threat for what it was.

00:25:19.252 --> 00:25:23.875
Carrillo had both the resources and power to significantly cut them out of the United States market,

00:25:24.636 --> 00:25:28.699
a market that consumes an estimated 400 tons of cocaine every year.

00:25:29.739 --> 00:25:30.180
In the end,

00:25:30.181 --> 00:25:35.723
the Colombians relinquished part of the distribution network in New York City to the Mexican drug lord.

00:25:36.524 --> 00:25:45.210
There was a point in the mid-90s in which Amado could traffic cocaine all along the entire nearly 2,000 mile long border.

00:25:45.914 --> 00:25:49.997
He could even go through Tijuana up into San Diego and Los Angeles,

00:25:50.197 --> 00:25:51.898
even though some of his bitter rivals are there,

00:25:51.918 --> 00:25:53.379
the Arellano Felix gang,

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:55.861
notorious for violence.

00:25:56.221 --> 00:25:56.361
But

00:25:57.342 --> 00:25:58.523
Amado was always a peacemaker.

00:25:59.443 --> 00:26:00.004
He believed,

00:26:00.504 --> 00:26:00.744
you know,

00:26:00.804 --> 00:26:02.726
as Miguel Angel Felix Gallardo taught him,

00:26:03.146 --> 00:26:08.090
that it's much better to work with your brother trafficker and make money than to kill him.

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:09.350
If you've got to kill him,

00:26:09.351 --> 00:26:09.951
you've got to kill him.

00:26:10.571 --> 00:26:12.573
But work together and you can just make a mint.

00:26:13.093 --> 00:26:14.294
And that's what he did for many years.

00:26:14.494 --> 00:26:15.775
He created a monster,

00:26:15.895 --> 00:26:16.296
I think.

00:26:17.136 --> 00:26:17.837
Before that,

00:26:17.917 --> 00:26:19.399
Mexico had always had four,

00:26:19.479 --> 00:26:19.819
five,

00:26:19.919 --> 00:26:20.139
six,

00:26:20.239 --> 00:26:20.540
seven,

00:26:20.660 --> 00:26:21.560
ten cartels,

00:26:21.561 --> 00:26:22.401
who the hell knows.

00:26:23.082 --> 00:26:26.845
And essentially the government could always stay on top by playing one against the other.

00:26:27.706 --> 00:26:30.949
He consolidated and became as big as the government.

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:32.150
At every turn,

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:36.534
DEA and other agencies were stymied by a lack of support from their Mexican counterparts.

00:26:37.475 --> 00:26:40.618
Carrillo's protection extended high into the echelons of power.

00:26:42.286 --> 00:26:46.707
Dramatic evidence of Carrillo's top-level collusion surfaced a few years later in U.S.

00:26:46.787 --> 00:26:47.448
federal court.

00:26:48.248 --> 00:26:49.088
Raul Macias,

00:26:49.288 --> 00:26:51.749
formerly of the Mexican Federal Judicial Police,

00:26:51.889 --> 00:26:58.571
testified that the local highway patrol had intercepted one of Carrillo's infamous air shipments in the northern state of Zacatecas.

00:26:58.871 --> 00:27:02.292
A huge full-body jet put down on a dirt runway.

00:27:02.293 --> 00:27:05.153
It was like a 727 or 707.

00:27:05.533 --> 00:27:06.153
No seats,

00:27:06.213 --> 00:27:07.013
full of cocaine.

00:27:07.433 --> 00:27:08.934
Typical Carrillo operation.

00:27:10.254 --> 00:27:10.774
Wait up.

00:27:10.775 --> 00:27:14.555
Macias testified that when he and the other federales arrived on the scene,

00:27:14.655 --> 00:27:17.916
his commanding officer ordered the highway patrolman to stand down.

00:27:18.076 --> 00:27:18.837
Somos federales.

00:27:18.857 --> 00:27:20.577
Vamos a tomar control de la situación.

00:27:22.098 --> 00:27:25.018
Macias then helped load the contraband onto waiting trucks.

00:27:25.738 --> 00:27:28.739
Macias also testified that inside police headquarters,

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:32.300
he was ordered to replace the cocaine with wood and other combustibles.

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:33.741
The cocaine,

00:27:33.761 --> 00:27:34.221
meanwhile,

00:27:34.241 --> 00:27:35.701
was loaded onto a large truck,

00:27:36.001 --> 00:27:38.542
presumably for delivery to one of Carrillo's underlings.

00:27:39.514 --> 00:27:40.675
The wood was then burned,

00:27:41.115 --> 00:27:44.979
masquerading as cocaine in a public ceremony of destruction.

00:27:45.719 --> 00:27:50.163
This exemplifies the type of operations that Amado Carrillo Fuentes had.

00:27:50.564 --> 00:27:53.966
I can guarantee you that none of the 10 tons were destroyed,

00:27:54.187 --> 00:27:54.467
okay?

00:27:55.288 --> 00:27:55.768
Eventually,

00:27:55.808 --> 00:27:58.711
they found themselves back into the American market.

00:27:59.751 --> 00:28:07.278
Everything we shared with the Mexican government through the drug czar would immediately be reported to Amado Carrillo Fuentes.

00:28:07.442 --> 00:28:08.903
In the spring of 96,

00:28:09.043 --> 00:28:15.706
somebody in DEA shared informants with somebody in the Mexican War on Drugs.

00:28:17.347 --> 00:28:18.967
And for three months after that,

00:28:19.127 --> 00:28:20.948
bodies littered the streets of War.

00:28:22.549 --> 00:28:25.850
They were instantly translating all the names given to the cartel.

00:28:29.272 --> 00:28:29.552
U.S.

00:28:29.632 --> 00:28:33.254
Customs agencies are set close to a million pounds of narcotics every year.

00:28:36.486 --> 00:28:40.470
The agency has had great success with its domestic air interdiction program,

00:28:40.471 --> 00:28:42.572
which monitors air traffic across the nation.

00:28:43.433 --> 00:28:46.136
When customs identifies an illegal airborne shipment,

00:28:46.517 --> 00:28:47.738
agents go on the offensive,

00:28:47.938 --> 00:28:50.821
tracking and chasing suspects until they are forced to land.

00:28:56.722 --> 00:28:57.822
The border crossing.

00:28:57.842 --> 00:28:58.402
Gamma ray,

00:28:58.663 --> 00:28:59.343
max scatter,

00:28:59.463 --> 00:29:04.264
x-ray imaging devices peer deep into the vehicles to expose expertly hidden narcotics.

00:29:05.684 --> 00:29:10.146
And even with the latest technologies employed to stem the flow of drugs into the United States,

00:29:10.506 --> 00:29:11.466
weak links remain.

00:29:11.866 --> 00:29:15.727
The border with the most traffic on Earth is the United States and Mexico.

00:29:16.267 --> 00:29:19.268
A quarter of a billion human beings cross it every year.

00:29:20.148 --> 00:29:21.389
There is no way to police it.

00:29:22.069 --> 00:29:22.329
U.S.

00:29:22.409 --> 00:29:24.269
Customs doesn't inspect 10%

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:25.930
of the vehicles coming in.

00:29:26.490 --> 00:29:27.494
What that means is,

00:29:28.157 --> 00:29:30.165
if you just threw the dope in the trunk and said,

00:29:30.166 --> 00:29:30.807
the hell with it,

00:29:30.848 --> 00:29:31.570
90%

00:29:31.571 --> 00:29:32.313
would get through.

00:29:32.792 --> 00:29:33.332
At the border,

00:29:33.392 --> 00:29:34.813
the odds favor the trafficker.

00:29:35.593 --> 00:29:37.273
When cocaine first enters Mexico,

00:29:37.413 --> 00:29:40.714
its wholesale price is approximately $2,000 per kilo,

00:29:40.874 --> 00:29:42.695
equal to about 2.2 pounds.

00:29:43.635 --> 00:29:44.976
By the time it reaches the border,

00:29:45.016 --> 00:29:47.996
that same cocaine is worth approximately $5,000.

00:29:48.817 --> 00:29:49.837
Once it crosses a border,

00:29:50.297 --> 00:29:55.499
the cocaine can fetch up to $20,000 wholesale with a street price that is potentially much higher.

00:29:56.699 --> 00:29:59.420
Even if you assume a remarkable confiscation rate of 50%.

00:30:00.404 --> 00:30:04.867
The cartel still clears 200 to 500 percent profit.

00:30:04.868 --> 00:30:07.368
And when you factor in the potential for corruption of U.S.

00:30:07.388 --> 00:30:07.848
officials,

00:30:08.369 --> 00:30:10.050
the odds improve even further.

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:11.270
There's documentation,

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:20.616
there's cases where inspectors have been paid up to a million dollars to look the other way as a load of cocaine goes through the port.

00:30:21.116 --> 00:30:24.518
If you look at a checkpoint on the border and there's 30 guys working there,

00:30:25.258 --> 00:30:26.859
you'd have to be a fool to buy 30.

00:30:26.959 --> 00:30:27.880
You only need one.

00:30:29.876 --> 00:30:32.058
Just run it when he's on or she's on.

00:30:32.799 --> 00:30:33.960
You don't have to corrupt many.

00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:36.142
I'll tell you what you do next then.

00:30:36.502 --> 00:30:37.583
To protect your boy,

00:30:38.043 --> 00:30:39.405
you throw that guy a load.

00:30:39.985 --> 00:30:42.848
You say there's a buick coming through with 200 pounds of coke in the trunk,

00:30:43.768 --> 00:30:44.649
so he looks clean.

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:46.471
That's not all the time.

00:30:47.392 --> 00:30:49.333
That's because the money's too big in drugs.

00:30:49.414 --> 00:30:52.476
Drugs corrupts everything and everyone.

00:31:00.436 --> 00:31:03.298
The Juarez cartel had only one consistent vulnerability,

00:31:03.759 --> 00:31:04.479
his cash flow.

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:09.103
Until the gargantuan profits had been laundered into legitimate looking dollars,

00:31:09.544 --> 00:31:10.925
Carrillo couldn't touch them.

00:31:11.705 --> 00:31:13.567
Drug money is not hiding in the ground in

00:31:14.087 --> 00:31:14.828
55-gallon drums.

00:31:15.729 --> 00:31:18.471
Drug money is in commerce with the rest of the money.

00:31:19.352 --> 00:31:26.518
It gets there because money launderers use the same facilities that legitimate corporations use who earn money.

00:31:26.938 --> 00:31:29.200
The laundering process was surprisingly simple.

00:31:29.544 --> 00:31:31.985
By secret arrangement between trafficker and banker,

00:31:32.285 --> 00:31:34.605
cash generated from drug sales in the U.S.

00:31:34.685 --> 00:31:39.367
was smuggled back into Mexico and deposited in any one of several Mexican banks.

00:31:39.667 --> 00:31:43.748
The bank would then issue the trafficker or his representative bank drafts in U.S.

00:31:43.848 --> 00:31:46.969
dollars drawn on the Mexican bank's accounts at major U.S.

00:31:47.049 --> 00:31:47.409
banks.

00:31:47.589 --> 00:31:53.210
The cash itself would then make its way back across the border by armored car and be deposited in the U.S.

00:31:53.771 --> 00:31:58.712
Bank drafts were then free to be laundered into legitimate business and real estate holdings around the world.

00:31:59.076 --> 00:31:59.896
San Antonio,

00:32:00.076 --> 00:32:09.580
Texas has a $3 billion surplus every year in its banking system that cannot be accounted by any calculus in a legitimate economy.

00:32:10.041 --> 00:32:12.001
Miami's $25 billion.

00:32:12.342 --> 00:32:15.363
El Paso's $750 million.

00:32:15.364 --> 00:32:17.664
If you wanted to hurt drugs by hurting money,

00:32:17.924 --> 00:32:19.404
you'd hurt it on both sides.

00:32:20.165 --> 00:32:21.565
But one side can bite you,

00:32:22.306 --> 00:32:23.866
meaning banking in this country.

00:32:24.186 --> 00:32:27.968
The biggest lobby for the NAFTA treaty didn't exist in Mexico.

00:32:28.540 --> 00:32:30.462
It existed in the U.S.

00:32:30.662 --> 00:32:39.750
financial institutions because they were the beneficiaries of all the money the Mexican banks and corporations would bring to the United States.

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:42.773
They're investing it overnight and earning from it.

00:32:42.774 --> 00:32:43.613
At his peak,

00:32:44.094 --> 00:32:47.036
Carrillo was making an estimated $10 billion a year.

00:32:47.797 --> 00:32:48.718
In that same time,

00:32:48.719 --> 00:32:52.641
it is believed he was paying $500 to $800 million in bribes.

00:32:53.722 --> 00:32:55.484
Having risen to the top of his profession,

00:32:55.968 --> 00:32:59.350
There is evidence that the Lord of the Skies was looking for a way out.

00:32:59.811 --> 00:33:03.593
In January of 1997,

00:33:03.594 --> 00:33:04.254
Amado Creel Fuentes,

00:33:04.314 --> 00:33:05.255
through an emissary,

00:33:06.035 --> 00:33:08.157
sent a message to the president of Mexico,

00:33:08.277 --> 00:33:09.257
Ernesto Cedillo.

00:33:10.018 --> 00:33:10.518
He said,

00:33:10.959 --> 00:33:12.820
I want you to stop harassing me.

00:33:13.420 --> 00:33:15.542
I want you to leave my business alone.

00:33:15.862 --> 00:33:17.944
I want you to leave my family alone.

00:33:18.424 --> 00:33:19.505
If you do that,

00:33:20.405 --> 00:33:22.567
I won't dump any dope in this country.

00:33:23.507 --> 00:33:24.208
If you don't.

00:33:24.428 --> 00:33:27.109
I'll leave this country and your economy will collapse.

00:33:27.409 --> 00:33:28.629
And that's absolutely true,

00:33:28.649 --> 00:33:29.129
it would.

00:33:29.769 --> 00:33:31.670
Because in 1997,

00:33:31.671 --> 00:33:33.731
Pemex was the biggest legal export.

00:33:33.791 --> 00:33:34.991
Petroleum in Mexico,

00:33:35.671 --> 00:33:37.572
it earned $7 to $8 billion a year.

00:33:38.012 --> 00:33:39.512
And in 1997,

00:33:39.552 --> 00:33:40.392
that same year,

00:33:41.133 --> 00:33:41.833
cocaine,

00:33:41.893 --> 00:33:42.453
marijuana,

00:33:42.513 --> 00:33:42.973
heroin,

00:33:43.233 --> 00:33:45.754
was earning Mexico $30 to $32 billion.

00:33:46.334 --> 00:33:51.455
The elder mater may have been in response to the January 6th raid on his family's compound in Sinaloa.

00:33:52.216 --> 00:33:53.876
Carrillo's sister was getting married.

00:33:53.996 --> 00:33:55.477
and he had flown home for the occasion.

00:33:56.278 --> 00:33:59.981
The police intended to capture the drug lord while he was relatively unprotected,

00:34:00.521 --> 00:34:04.304
but an anonymous phone called tipped Carrillo off before the police could arrive.

00:34:04.945 --> 00:34:08.068
Given how much money he was said to be paying the government for protection,

00:34:08.568 --> 00:34:11.350
it is likely Carrillo viewed the raid as an insult.

00:34:11.550 --> 00:34:11.871
Amado,

00:34:12.891 --> 00:34:14.713
according to his lawyer and others,

00:34:15.654 --> 00:34:16.835
was a real nationalist.

00:34:19.117 --> 00:34:22.419
He said that someone had to get their hands dirty for the sake of Mexico,

00:34:22.699 --> 00:34:23.240
and if that meant...

00:34:23.420 --> 00:34:42.966
moving drugs well let's move them if it helps the economy if it helps mexico this wedding video is some of the only known footage of amado carillo fuentes he looks like hell he looks like a ceo who spent three weeks on a corporate merger and hasn't slept and has been living off either whiskey or cocaine and

00:34:42.967 --> 00:34:50.748
you can tell he's a man under pressure that it's getting to him it's like watching scarface the last reel

00:34:52.854 --> 00:34:54.314
In February of 97,

00:34:54.315 --> 00:34:55.975
Carrillo's empire began to shatter.

00:34:57.196 --> 00:34:59.016
General Jesus Gutierrez Rebollo,

00:34:59.376 --> 00:35:01.817
recently appointed Mexico's newest anti-drug czar,

00:35:02.358 --> 00:35:05.939
was discovered to be living in a luxury apartment paid for by Carrillo.

00:35:06.399 --> 00:35:07.340
It was a scandal,

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:08.380
a huge scandal,

00:35:09.121 --> 00:35:09.361
you know,

00:35:09.362 --> 00:35:15.143
to have the head of your anti-drug agency to be on the payroll of the country's biggest drug trafficker.

00:35:15.383 --> 00:35:19.325
It's kind of like having somebody working at Los Alamos and giving a...

00:35:19.953 --> 00:35:21.314
All of our nuclear secrets to the

00:35:22.255 --> 00:35:26.399
Chinese. The general's arrest came a mere two weeks after having been in Washington,

00:35:26.459 --> 00:35:26.739
D.C.

00:35:26.899 --> 00:35:27.720
to meet with U.S.

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:32.364
drug czar Barry McCaffrey and discuss confidential counter-narcotics operations.

00:35:32.964 --> 00:35:33.605
To the press,

00:35:33.745 --> 00:35:38.509
McCaffrey even expressed his belief that the general was an officer of highest integrity.

00:35:39.330 --> 00:35:40.230
After he was appointed,

00:35:40.731 --> 00:35:43.153
General Gutierrez-Rebollo had a meeting with reporters.

00:35:44.294 --> 00:35:44.634
He said,

00:35:44.654 --> 00:35:44.894
you know,

00:35:45.315 --> 00:35:47.897
the problem isn't these big drug cartels in Mexico.

00:35:48.437 --> 00:35:50.841
You gotta be real concerned about these little cartels,

00:35:51.221 --> 00:35:52.182
the cartelitos.

00:35:52.543 --> 00:35:55.186
They're the ones that are really much more important than the big guys.

00:35:55.286 --> 00:35:56.208
Because if you add them all up,

00:35:56.368 --> 00:35:56.768
they're bigger.

00:35:57.429 --> 00:35:58.971
He was telegraphing his message.

00:35:59.091 --> 00:35:59.592
He was saying,

00:35:59.772 --> 00:36:00.733
I'm not going after Amado.

00:36:08.433 --> 00:36:08.893
Every year,

00:36:09.033 --> 00:36:14.636
the United States scrutinizes cooperative anti-drug efforts around the world in a process known as certification.

00:36:14.876 --> 00:36:17.457
Countries that fail the review face trade penalties.

00:36:17.617 --> 00:36:26.480
The government and the media have unearthed new information confirming previous suspicions about the extent of narco-corruption within Mexican institutions.

00:36:26.881 --> 00:36:33.343
This connection between Gutierrez and Amado was exposed by the government of Mexico itself.

00:36:34.284 --> 00:36:37.285
Mexico's failure to confront these cartels

00:36:37.761 --> 00:36:44.186
does not protect a single child on any street of any city in the United States of America.

00:36:44.326 --> 00:36:46.008
We stand by the decision,

00:36:46.088 --> 00:36:46.488
obviously,

00:36:46.628 --> 00:36:48.510
to have the United States certify

00:36:49.030 --> 00:36:54.074
Mexico, and we're very much opposed to the efforts in Congress to reverse that decision,

00:36:54.575 --> 00:37:00.039
because we don't see how it helps the United States to walk away from Mexico when both of us are together,

00:37:00.419 --> 00:37:02.781
working together in a fight in the drug wars.

00:37:03.422 --> 00:37:04.943
It's almost become a ritual now.

00:37:05.063 --> 00:37:07.085
Every time certification comes up in February,

00:37:08.001 --> 00:37:09.682
The Mexicans end up offering something,

00:37:09.683 --> 00:37:10.382
a sacrifice.

00:37:10.582 --> 00:37:15.464
It was a tremendous shock to all of us to have this

00:37:15.824 --> 00:37:18.986
General Officer Gutierrez-Rabollo revealed,

00:37:20.066 --> 00:37:22.607
alleged to be a criminal.

00:37:22.608 --> 00:37:26.329
There's two things about certification I think everybody should know.

00:37:26.609 --> 00:37:26.729
One,

00:37:26.769 --> 00:37:27.989
it's a total fraud.

00:37:28.009 --> 00:37:29.790
It doesn't change anything in Mexico.

00:37:30.670 --> 00:37:31.130
And two,

00:37:31.371 --> 00:37:33.031
it's a preposterous action.

00:37:33.731 --> 00:37:36.913
The country that should be decertified is the United States.

00:37:37.533 --> 00:37:37.873
5%

00:37:38.113 --> 00:37:42.815
of the world's population and a nose that sucks up half the world's cocaine.

00:37:43.455 --> 00:37:47.796
It is just a damn lie to say the Mexicans are ruining our country with drugs.

00:37:49.117 --> 00:37:52.998
Any demand for drugs will create a market.

00:37:53.778 --> 00:37:56.259
The drugs will be here as long as we want to buy them.

00:37:56.959 --> 00:37:58.160
If it's not the Mexicans,

00:37:58.200 --> 00:37:59.600
somebody else will bring them.

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:01.481
But this is our problem,

00:38:01.581 --> 00:38:02.341
not theirs.

00:38:04.741 --> 00:38:05.982
After the general's arrest,

00:38:06.062 --> 00:38:07.183
Carrillo went underground.

00:38:08.063 --> 00:38:08.864
During this time,

00:38:08.865 --> 00:38:10.345
he was reportedly seen in Cuba,

00:38:10.645 --> 00:38:11.126
in Europe,

00:38:11.186 --> 00:38:12.406
and as far away as Russia.

00:38:13.347 --> 00:38:18.030
Officials eventually learned that he was preparing to move his operations out of the country and down to Chile.

00:38:18.971 --> 00:38:20.752
If he was no longer welcome in Mexico,

00:38:20.852 --> 00:38:21.493
it was recent,

00:38:21.513 --> 00:38:25.816
then Carrillo would then run his drug empire from the very bottom of the world.

00:38:27.057 --> 00:38:31.260
But Carrillo surprised everyone when he returned to Mexico City in July of 97,

00:38:31.660 --> 00:38:33.321
ready to undergo plastic surgery.

00:38:34.189 --> 00:38:36.651
He checked into the hospital under an assumed name.

00:38:38.773 --> 00:38:39.874
For over nine hours,

00:38:39.894 --> 00:38:43.037
a team of four doctors operated on Carrillo.

00:38:43.038 --> 00:38:45.659
Radical liposuction removed 20 pounds of fat.

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:48.341
Facial sculpting altered his appearance.

00:38:49.202 --> 00:38:49.683
In the end,

00:38:49.723 --> 00:38:50.703
however,

00:38:50.704 --> 00:38:55.267
an overdose of the tranquilizer Dormicum kept Carrillo from ever leaving the table.

00:38:56.589 --> 00:39:00.132
When officials learned that Carrillo had died under such bizarre circumstances,

00:39:00.312 --> 00:39:01.553
few were willing to believe it.

00:39:02.641 --> 00:39:03.202
After all,

00:39:03.222 --> 00:39:08.706
it wouldn't have been the first time a fugitive had faked his own death by leaving an unrecognizable corpse.

00:39:16.872 --> 00:39:19.214
It so happens that the DEA or the FBI,

00:39:19.254 --> 00:39:19.674
I'm not sure,

00:39:19.714 --> 00:39:23.297
but a couple of agents were allowed to take fingerprints of the body.

00:39:24.097 --> 00:39:27.180
And a comparison was made with the fingerprint,

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:27.840
the thumbprint,

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:31.683
of Amato's border crossing card that was issued to him in 1935.

00:39:32.583 --> 00:39:34.104
This allowed Thomas Constantine,

00:39:34.164 --> 00:39:35.685
who was DBA director at the time,

00:39:36.545 --> 00:39:37.066
to announce,

00:39:37.086 --> 00:39:38.146
even before the Mexicans did,

00:39:38.166 --> 00:39:39.227
that it wasn't Pat Amato.

00:39:39.367 --> 00:39:40.347
And I think that's correct.

00:39:41.908 --> 00:39:42.789
Four months later,

00:39:43.049 --> 00:39:48.552
the bodies of Carrillo's doctors were discovered stuffed inside oil guns along the side of a deserted highway.

00:39:49.312 --> 00:39:50.933
They had been extensively tortured.

00:39:51.385 --> 00:39:55.568
It's widely believed that the family members did this to the doctors.

00:39:56.769 --> 00:39:57.489
That's very likely.

00:39:57.529 --> 00:40:00.691
The family wanted to find out why Amato died,

00:40:01.352 --> 00:40:04.774
and so they tortured people to death to try to find out what the truth is.

00:40:06.535 --> 00:40:07.436
Through sources,

00:40:08.697 --> 00:40:14.220
Nova person saw the body the night he died and said the sutures weren't even stitched.

00:40:15.181 --> 00:40:15.301
Well,

00:40:15.321 --> 00:40:17.383
plastic surgeons closed the sutures.

00:40:17.863 --> 00:40:19.544
You're paying to not have scars.

00:40:19.584 --> 00:40:20.365
That's the whole idea,

00:40:20.385 --> 00:40:21.045
to change your...

00:40:21.065 --> 00:40:21.465
appearance,

00:40:21.946 --> 00:40:25.409
which would suggest somebody deliberately killed him by injection.

00:40:25.709 --> 00:40:26.630
The question there is,

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:27.991
who injected the chemical?

00:40:29.352 --> 00:40:30.593
Did the government kill him,

00:40:31.073 --> 00:40:32.555
members of his own cartel,

00:40:33.035 --> 00:40:34.156
another cartel,

00:40:34.676 --> 00:40:36.398
American law enforcement,

00:40:36.558 --> 00:40:37.318
or the CIA?

00:40:37.739 --> 00:40:39.861
Mexican authorities are convinced it was murder,

00:40:39.881 --> 00:40:41.302
but they haven't offered any evidence.

00:40:41.662 --> 00:40:44.404
My theory is that he really did die under plastic surgery.

00:40:45.005 --> 00:40:47.387
He had been using crack cocaine for at least 10 years.

00:40:48.147 --> 00:40:49.869
That has a tendency to degenerate.

00:40:50.625 --> 00:40:51.085
Your organs,

00:40:51.086 --> 00:40:51.506
your heart,

00:40:51.886 --> 00:40:52.347
your liver.

00:40:52.727 --> 00:40:53.427
What the truth is,

00:40:54.148 --> 00:40:55.489
probably the Carrillo family,

00:40:56.470 --> 00:40:57.711
they're the only ones who will ever know.

00:41:05.838 --> 00:41:09.841
Carrillo's sudden death left a power vacuum in the Mexican underworld.

00:41:09.842 --> 00:41:11.402
Within the Juarez cartel itself,

00:41:11.623 --> 00:41:13.944
no clear replacement for Carrillo has yet emerged.

00:41:14.865 --> 00:41:16.687
After the death of Amaro Carrillo Fuentes,

00:41:17.067 --> 00:41:18.708
a drug war in a true sense.

00:41:19.369 --> 00:41:28.136
of narco-traffickers fighting each other emerged.

00:41:28.137 --> 00:41:30.078
We like to say Mexico is relatively quiet.

00:41:30.098 --> 00:41:34.462
The FBI figures that there have been a thousand drug murders in Mexico in the last 24 months.

00:41:34.463 --> 00:41:35.222
I think that's wrong.

00:41:38.689 --> 00:41:41.010
One of the emerging figures is a guy by the name of

00:41:41.471 --> 00:41:43.672
Juan Jose Espargosa Moreno.

00:41:44.272 --> 00:41:45.433
His nickname is El Azul,

00:41:45.913 --> 00:41:47.114
blue that means.

00:41:47.274 --> 00:41:53.658
He got that because his skin is so dark it resembles the blue-black sheen of a polished gun barrel.

00:41:53.659 --> 00:41:59.101
El Azul is one of the last old-time traffickers who really believes in peace and unity.

00:41:59.882 --> 00:42:01.823
And according to the FBI and DEA,

00:42:02.023 --> 00:42:04.965
he's back and he's big in the Juarez cartel.

00:42:05.229 --> 00:42:05.629
Frankly,

00:42:05.669 --> 00:42:07.450
I think when the new Amado arrives,

00:42:07.451 --> 00:42:08.631
we'll never know his name,

00:42:08.991 --> 00:42:11.012
because he doesn't want to become dead like Amado.

00:42:11.653 --> 00:42:15.855
It has been said that if Amado Carillo Fuentes had been offered the presidency of Mexico,

00:42:16.415 --> 00:42:18.216
he would have considered it a demotion.

00:42:19.557 --> 00:42:22.759
And whether powerful drug lords hold sway over the Mexican government,

00:42:23.319 --> 00:42:25.960
or the Mexican government hold sway over the drug lords,

00:42:26.541 --> 00:42:27.021
in the end,

00:42:27.041 --> 00:42:30.223
the citizens of Mexico are left with the same familiar question.

00:42:31.123 --> 00:42:32.684
Plata o plomo?

00:42:33.585 --> 00:42:33.985
Silver?

00:42:34.601 --> 00:42:35.061
Or lead?

00:42:36.282 --> 00:42:38.884
Will they turn a blind eye to the corruption and complicity,

00:42:39.084 --> 00:42:41.965
or will they fight the Amaro Carrillo Fuenteses of the world?

00:42:42.426 --> 00:42:43.026
We have,

00:42:43.027 --> 00:42:43.566
in the past,

00:42:43.646 --> 00:42:45.627
won major battles,

00:42:46.448 --> 00:42:48.909
but we're still a long ways from winning the war.

00:42:49.229 --> 00:42:53.052
Amaro Carrillo died in July of 1997.

00:42:53.053 --> 00:42:55.033
I haven't run into a single American who says,

00:42:55.053 --> 00:42:57.334
I can't find any coke and I can't afford it.

00:42:57.654 --> 00:42:59.075
Heroin's a damn cheap town,

00:42:59.115 --> 00:43:00.236
people won't inject it.

00:43:01.556 --> 00:43:02.397
They just snort it.

00:43:03.401 --> 00:43:04.622
His death meant nothing.

00:43:04.642 --> 00:43:06.623
It was like the death of the head of General Motors.

00:43:06.624 --> 00:43:09.765
The Chevys just keep rolling off the line.

00:43:09.805 --> 00:43:11.506
But you have to give the devil his due.

00:43:11.946 --> 00:43:13.367
He built something durable.

00:43:14.448 --> 00:43:14.968
In Mexico,

00:43:14.969 --> 00:43:16.889
if you ask people about Amado Carrillo,

00:43:17.529 --> 00:43:19.671
ordinary Joe Sixpack on the street is going to say,

00:43:19.751 --> 00:43:19.871
well,

00:43:19.891 --> 00:43:20.791
he's still alive.

00:43:21.512 --> 00:43:21.752
You know,

00:43:21.972 --> 00:43:23.773
there's such belief in his myth,

00:43:23.893 --> 00:43:24.394
his power,

00:43:24.474 --> 00:43:25.034
his legend,

00:43:25.334 --> 00:43:27.695
that some Mexicans think he couldn't be dead.

00:43:28.396 --> 00:43:29.356
He's the lord of the skies.

00:43:29.357 --> 00:43:30.037
He can't die.

